<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ §01 -->
<section id="s-waste" data-code="waste">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Static Tree Problem</div>
<h2>Uniform Branching Wastes Your Draft Budget</h2>

<p>
  Speculative decoding speeds up LLM inference by having a small, cheap <em>draft model</em> propose
  several tokens at once, then having the large target model verify all of them in a single forward pass.
  The more tokens the target model accepts per pass, the bigger the speedup.
</p>
<p>
  To increase acceptance, methods like EAGLE and Medusa moved from single chains to <em>draft trees</em>:
  rather than one linear sequence of draft tokens, the drafter generates multiple branching paths.
  If the first branch gets rejected, a sibling branch may still be accepted. More paths = more
  chances to get it right.
</p>
<p>
  But there's a hidden assumption baked into how these trees are built: every position in the tree gets
  the same number of branches. Position 1 always spawns <em>k</em> children. Position 2 always spawns
  <em>k</em> children. This is a static tree — its shape never changes regardless of the input.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">static vs. adaptive tree — same draft budget, different allocation</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a context to compare</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="tree-controls">
      <button class="tree-btn active" id="ctx-easy">Context: "10 + 2 ="</button>
      <button class="tree-btn" id="ctx-hard">Context: "The meeting will probably"</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="tree-canvas" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block;"></canvas>
    <div class="tree-legend">
      <span><span class="tree-legend-dot" style="background:var(--purple)"></span>selected node</span>
      <span><span class="tree-legend-dot" style="background:rgba(16,185,129,.8)"></span>high confidence (&gt;0.7)</span>
      <span><span class="tree-legend-dot" style="background:rgba(245,158,11,.8)"></span>medium confidence (0.3–0.7)</span>
      <span><span class="tree-legend-dot" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,.8)"></span>low confidence (&lt;0.3)</span>
      <span><span class="tree-legend-dot" style="background:rgba(255,255,255,.12)"></span>pruned (not in adaptive tree)</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The problem: when the next token is obvious — say, the model is completing <code>"10 + 2 ="</code>
  and the answer is almost certainly <code>"12"</code> — the static tree still spawns a sibling branch
  for <code>"11"</code>, <code>"13"</code>, etc. These siblings will almost never be accepted.
  You're spending draft budget on tokens that have near-zero probability of surviving verification.
</p>
<p>
  Conversely, when the context is genuinely ambiguous — say <code>"The meeting will probably"</code>
  could continue with <code>"run"</code>, <code>"be"</code>, <code>"last"</code>, <code>"end"</code>,
  or <code>"go"</code> — the static tree might not expand wide enough to cover the plausible completions.
</p>
<p>
  The right answer is obvious in retrospect: <strong>branch more when uncertain, branch less when
  confident</strong>. But implementing it requires a cheap, reliable signal for uncertainty. That signal
  already exists — the draft model computes it automatically at every step.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ §02 -->
<section id="s-primer" data-code="primer">
<div class="section-no">02 — Spec-Decoding Primer</div>
<h2>Draft, Tree, Verify: How Speculative Decoding Works</h2>

<p>
  Before getting to confidence-guided trees, it helps to have the mechanics clearly in mind.
  Speculative decoding works in alternating cycles:
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">speculative decoding — one cycle</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">step through the phases</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="spec-steps">
      <div class="step-box active">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Draft: small model generates candidates</div>
          <div class="step-desc">
            A cheap draft model (e.g., 1B params) autoregressively generates K candidate tokens starting
            from the current context. In tree-based methods, it generates a branching tree of candidates
            — depth D, branching factor k at each position — producing up to k<sup>D</sup> leaf paths.
            For EAGLE, the drafter works at the <em>feature level</em> (second-to-top layer) using
            context embeddings from the target LLM, making its predictions tightly coupled to the
            target model's distribution.
          </div>
          <div class="step-code">draft_tree = drafter.generate_tree(prefix, depth=4, k=2)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Tree Attention: flatten tree with causal mask</div>
          <div class="step-desc">
            The tree is flattened into a 1D token sequence for a single forward pass through the big
            model. The attention mask is customized: each token can only attend to its ancestor nodes
            (not siblings or cousins). This means all branches are scored simultaneously in one pass —
            the same cost as processing a single chain of length |tree|.
          </div>
          <div class="step-code">tokens, mask = flatten_tree(draft_tree)  # tree attention</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Verify: target model scores all candidates at once</div>
          <div class="step-desc">
            The large model (e.g., Vicuna 13B) processes the flattened tree in one forward pass and
            returns logits for every candidate token. This is the expensive step — but because multiple
            candidates are checked simultaneously, each forward pass amortizes its cost over potentially
            3–5 accepted tokens.
          </div>
          <div class="step-code">target_logits = big_model(tokens, attn_mask=mask)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Accept: token-by-token accept/reject from root</div>
          <div class="step-desc">
            Starting from the root, each candidate token t̂ is accepted with probability
            min(1, p_big(t̂) / p_draft(t̂)). Acceptance is sequential: if a token is rejected,
            all its descendants are discarded but sibling branches are attempted. The prefix is
            extended by all accepted tokens, then the cycle repeats. Output distribution is
            provably identical to greedy/sampling from the big model alone.
          </div>
          <div class="step-code">accepted = accept_reject(target_logits, draft_probs)  # lossless</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="step-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <span id="step-prog" style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);font-family:var(--ff-mono)">Step 1 of 4</span>
      <button id="step-next">Next →</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The key metric is the <strong>average acceptance length τ</strong>: how many tokens are accepted per
  draft-verify cycle. If τ = 4.5, the big model only needs to run once for every 4.5 tokens of output —
  compare that to 1.0 for vanilla autoregressive decoding. Speedup ratio ≈ τ (minus overhead of
  running the draft model).
</p>
<p>
  A static tree with depth 4 and branching factor 2 has 2⁴ = 16 leaf paths, but only about 6–8 nodes
  are typically reachable before a rejection. The question is: <em>which</em> nodes to include in
  the tree to maximize the expected τ?
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ §03 -->
<section id="s-calibration" data-code="calibration">
<div class="section-no">03 — Confidence = Acceptance Rate</div>
<h2>The Draft Model Already Knows Its Own Reliability</h2>

<p>
  To build an adaptive tree, you need a cheap estimate of how likely the target model is to
  accept each candidate token. The obvious approach — asking the target model — defeats the
  purpose: that's exactly what verification is for, and it requires a full forward pass.
</p>
<p>
  But EAGLE-2 discovered something remarkable: <strong>the draft model's confidence score
  (its own output probability for a token) is a near-perfect proxy for the target model's
  acceptance rate</strong>. No extra computation. No second model. The signal is already there.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">draft confidence vs. target acceptance rate — Vicuna 7B on Alpaca</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">points represent confidence score bins</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="cal-canvas" style="width:100%;height:280px;display:block;"></canvas>
    <div class="cal-badges">
      <span class="cal-badge" style="color:var(--cyan)">confidence 0.05 → acceptance ~0.04</span>
      <span class="cal-badge" style="color:var(--amber)">confidence 0.50 → acceptance ~0.52</span>
      <span class="cal-badge" style="color:var(--green)">confidence 0.95 → acceptance ~0.98</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The data (Figure 6 in the EAGLE-2 paper) shows an almost perfectly diagonal relationship between
  the draft model's confidence and the measured acceptance rate on held-out data. A draft token
  with 95% confidence gets accepted ~98% of the time. A draft token with 5% confidence gets
  accepted ~4% of the time.
</p>
<p>
  Why does this hold? Because EAGLE's draft model is trained specifically to mimic the target LLM's
  feature distribution. When the draft model is confident, it means it's in a region of token space
  where the target distribution is also concentrated. When the draft model is uncertain, the token
  space is genuinely ambiguous and the target model may well prefer a different token.
</p>
<p>
  This calibration is the linchpin of the entire approach. Without it, confidence scores would be
  meaningless for tree construction. With it, they become a free oracle for expected acceptance — and
  you can use them to reshape the draft tree without any additional computation.
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Core approximation</div>
  p<sub>accept</sub>(t̂<sub>i</sub>) = min(1, p<sub>big</sub>(t̂<sub>i</sub>) / p<sub>draft</sub>(t̂<sub>i</sub>))
  <span class="math-comment">   ← exact acceptance probability (requires target model)</span><br>
  ≈ c<sub>i</sub>  <span class="math-comment">   ← draft model's confidence score (free)</span><br><br>
  <span class="math-comment"># This approximation is empirically tight: Pearson r ≈ 0.995 on Alpaca+Vicuna 7B</span>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ §04 -->
<section id="s-algorithm" data-code="algorithm">
<div class="section-no">04 — The Algorithm</div>
<h2>Building the Tree: Value, Expand, Rerank</h2>

<p>
  Armed with the confidence-as-acceptance approximation, EAGLE-2 builds the draft tree in two phases:
  <strong>expand</strong> (grow the tree layer by layer) and <strong>rerank</strong> (select the best
  subset of nodes for verification).
</p>

<h3>The Path Value Vi</h3>
<p>
  A single confidence score tells you how likely <em>this</em> token is to be accepted — but for a
  token to contribute to speedup, <em>every token on the path from the root to it</em> must also be
  accepted (rejection of any ancestor discards all descendants). So what matters is the product of
  confidence scores along the whole path, called the <strong>path value V<sub>i</sub></strong>:
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Path value formula</div>
  <span class="math-var">V<sub>i</sub></span> = ∏<sub>t<sub>j</sub> ∈ Path(root, t<sub>i</sub>)</sub> p<sub>accept</sub>(t<sub>j</sub>)
  &nbsp;&nbsp;≈&nbsp;&nbsp; ∏<sub>t<sub>j</sub> ∈ Path(root, t<sub>i</sub>)</sub> <span class="math-var">c<sub>j</sub></span><br><br>
  <span class="math-comment"># V_i ≈ probability that token i is ever actually accepted and contributes to output</span><br>
  <span class="math-comment"># It monotonically decreases with depth — deeper nodes have lower V than their parent</span>
</div>

<p>
  The path value has a clean interpretation: it's approximately the probability that token t<sub>i</sub>
  actually makes it into the accepted output. A node with V = 0.34 will contribute about 0.34 accepted
  tokens in expectation. So to maximize the expected number of accepted tokens (= the speedup), you
  want to include exactly the nodes with the highest path values.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">EAGLE-2 tree construction — interactive example from the paper</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a node to highlight its path value</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="tree-controls">
      <button class="tree-btn active" id="phase-expand">Expand Phase (top-2)</button>
      <button class="tree-btn" id="phase-rerank">Rerank Phase (top-8)</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="algo-canvas" style="width:100%;height:320px;display:block;"></canvas>
    <div class="tree-legend">
      <span><span class="tree-legend-dot" style="background:rgba(245,158,11,.9)"></span>expanded in this phase</span>
      <span><span class="tree-legend-dot" style="background:rgba(16,185,129,.9)"></span>selected for verification</span>
      <span><span class="tree-legend-dot" style="background:rgba(255,255,255,.12)"></span>generated but not selected</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>Expand Phase</h3>
<p>
  The tree is grown one layer at a time. At each layer, the draft model has already computed
  confidence scores for all current leaf nodes. The algorithm selects the <strong>top-k nodes
  by path value</strong> and expands them — feeding them into the draft model to generate the
  next layer of candidates. This ensures expansion effort is spent where it matters most.
</p>
<p>
  For the concrete example from the paper: prefix "It" → the draft model produces candidates
  "is" (confidence 0.6), "has" (0.2), "was" (0.08), etc. Top-2 by path value expands "is"
  and "has". "Is" then generates "a" (0.8) and "the" (0.1); "has" generates "a" (0.7)
  and "to" (0.1). And so on deeper.
</p>

<h3>Rerank Phase</h3>
<p>
  After expansion, the tree contains more nodes than will fit in a single forward pass of the
  target model (for compute-efficiency reasons). The rerank phase selects the
  <strong>top-m nodes by path value</strong> across all nodes in the tree — not just the
  latest layer. The constraint is that the selected set must still form a connected tree (each
  selected node's ancestors must also be selected), which is guaranteed by V<sub>i</sub> ≤ V<sub>parent(i)</sub>.
</p>
<p>
  These top-m nodes are then flattened into a 1D sequence with a tree-structured attention mask
  and handed to the target model for verification. The rest of the draft tree is discarded.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ §05 -->
<section id="s-theory" data-code="theory">
<div class="section-no">05 — Why It's Optimal</div>
<h2>Maximizing Expected Accepted Tokens</h2>

<p>
  The confidence-guided approach isn't just intuitive — it's provably optimal for a specific
  objective. Given a fixed draft budget (total number of nodes m to submit to the verifier),
  which nodes should you include to maximize the expected number of accepted tokens?
</p>

<p>
  The answer: <strong>include exactly the m nodes with the highest path values V<sub>i</sub></strong>.
  This is because the contribution to expected accepted tokens from node t<sub>i</sub> is exactly
  V<sub>i</sub> — so a greedy selection by V<sub>i</sub> maximizes the total expected contribution.
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Expected accepted tokens = sum of path values</div>
  E[τ] = Σ<sub>i ∈ tree</sub> V<sub>i</sub>  = Σ<sub>i ∈ tree</sub> ∏<sub>t<sub>j</sub> ∈ Path(root, t<sub>i</sub>)</sub> c<sub>j</sub><br><br>
  <span class="math-comment"># Greedy selection: include top-m nodes by V_i to maximize E[τ]</span><br>
  <span class="math-comment"># This is a closed-form solution — no DP, no search needed</span>
</div>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">expected tokens accepted: uniform branching vs confidence-guided (same budget)</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">shows budget m = 4 to 20 nodes</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="theory-canvas" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block;"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The comparison is stark: uniform branching (always expand the same k candidates per position)
  locks in a fixed tree shape that may be suboptimal for the current context. Confidence-guided
  allocation flexibly moves nodes to wherever path values are highest — which means shallow
  nodes in high-confidence regions get trimmed and the budget is redirected to exploring
  branches in uncertain regions.
</p>

<h3>Connection to Entropy and Perplexity</h3>
<p>
  The "branch when uncertain" intuition maps directly onto information-theoretic quantities.
  A token position where the draft model assigns high confidence to one token has <strong>low
  entropy</strong> — the distribution is peaked. A position where confidence is spread across
  several candidates has <strong>high entropy</strong> — the distribution is flat.
</p>
<p>
  EAGLE-2's algorithm branches exactly at high-entropy (high-perplexity) positions: the top-k
  expansion naturally selects multiple candidates at positions where no single candidate dominates.
  When a single token has confidence near 1.0, its path value is nearly identical to its parent's,
  so the runner-up's path value is near-zero — the algorithm effectively ignores it.
</p>
<p>
  This also explains why static trees underperform on some tasks more than others. Mathematical
  reasoning (GSM8K) contains many forced continuations — once you've committed to a reasoning path,
  individual tokens are often uniquely determined. Static trees waste budget on impossible branches.
  Code generation (HumanEval) has high-entropy positions at API names and variable choices but
  very low entropy inside fixed syntactic patterns. Confidence-guided allocation captures both.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ §06 -->
<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">06 — Results</div>
<h2>4.26× on MT-Bench: The EAGLE Lineage</h2>

<p>
  EAGLE-2 was evaluated across six tasks (MT-bench, HumanEval, GSM8K, Alpaca, CNN/DM, Natural
  Questions) on three model families (Vicuna, LLaMA2-Chat, LLaMA3-Instruct). Compared to EAGLE-1's
  static trees, confidence-guided dynamic trees deliver <strong>20–40% additional speedup</strong>
  on every benchmark and model size.
</p>

