<p>
  The average acceptance length τ (hardware-independent) tells the same story. EAGLE-1 achieves
  τ ≈ 3.98 on MT-bench with Vicuna 13B; EAGLE-2 reaches τ ≈ 4.83 — almost a full extra token
  per verification cycle, without any change to the verifier or the draft model weights.
</p>

<h3>Ablation: Value vs. Confidence Score</h3>
<p>
  EAGLE-2 also ran an ablation comparing two expansion strategies:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>By confidence</strong>: expand the top-k nodes with the highest local confidence score (ignoring ancestors)</li>
  <li><strong>By value</strong>: expand the top-k nodes with the highest path value V<sub>i</sub> = product of ancestor confidences</li>
</ul>
<p>
  Expanding by path value consistently outperforms expanding by local confidence. A node with
  high local confidence but a low-confidence parent has a low probability of ever being accepted.
  Including such nodes wastes budget. The path value correctly accounts for this cascading
  dependency.
</p>

<h3>EAGLE-3 and Beyond</h3>
<p>
  EAGLE-3 (arXiv:2503.01840) pushed the design further: abandoning feature-level prediction in favor
  of direct token prediction with multi-layer feature fusion, and fixing a training data scalability
  bottleneck that had capped EAGLE-2's gains. The result: up to <strong>6.5×</strong> speedup — roughly
  1.4× over EAGLE-2 — with the same confidence-guided tree construction at its core. The idea of
  branching by uncertainty wasn't replaced; it was the foundation that later improvements built on.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
